{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1795543745,"nonce":"ZFxD7mFaPscUImh4rOLCDKPUBmM=","target":"57216bfaec988d9d175b48f05b13898009bf5505b87d5990d81c6514bcf76804","message":"Backward Euler could not march the conjugate gas: Euler equations (vector unknown `q = (rho, m, E)` with HLL), a scalar algebraic temperature `T = p/rho`, and conduction of `T` in the energy row. The stiffness probe failed with \"could not assemble the residual's Jacobian on the face graph\", with `limiter=minmod` and without. The example marched with `jno.solve.imex` instead.\n\n**Cause.** Not the gas model. A march inside `jax.jit` (every coupling window) has a traced state, so the sparsity of its Jacobian was decided at zeros plus 1 % noise. There `p/rho \u003c 0` in about half the cells, and the flux has complex wave speeds. The assembled tangent then missed the jvp by 10 % with minmod and 0.9 % without, at stencil radius 2, 3 and 4 alike.\n\n**Reproduce.** `tests/test_fvm_transient.py::test_a_gas_steps_implicitly_inside_a_compiled_program` marches a conducting gas with an algebraic temperature by backward Euler inside `jax.jit` in a closed box and checks that mass is kept to round-off.\n\n**Fix.** Commit fc0394ff in #345: an implicit march inside a compiled program probes its Jacobian at the initial state, at the stored parameter values (zeros where the problem has none). There the tangent matches the jvp to 2.6e-16 at radius 2. Eager marches already probed their concrete state and are unchanged. In the vessel tutorial the gas now steps by backward Euler in a coupling, one step per window with an acoustic CFL of about 5, and gives the same results as the IMEX version (pressure 3.000 to 2.809, wall up to 1.178, bore displacement 2.77e-2 against 2.71e-2 for IMEX).\n\n**Related.** The steady twin of this flaw is #346.","files":null}]}